;;; -*- Mode: Text -*-
;;; README.abclr2
#|

This example program is metalevel definitions for TimeWarp
algorithm[1], which is an algorithm for parallel discrete event
simulation.  In addition to rollback mechanism at metalevel, this
program has ability to control scheduling policy (for example, one
can use Lowest Virtual Time first scheduling).

As details of the algorithm are describe in [2] and [3], only how to
use of this program is shown here.

1. How to run this program.

First "cd" to this directory, then evaluate (inc "tw-load"). This
compiles all programs and then loads.  Loaded program is a "car wash
problem" (same as the one described in [4]).  Sending a message to
"starter" starts simulation in two kinds of scheduling policy (Lowest
LVT first and FIFO).  For example,
	[starter <== [:start [3 4 6] 10]]
starts a simulation with three attendants whose washing times are 3,
4, and 6, respectively, and 10 cars.


2. How to use this program for other simulations.

All files except for "tw-load", "tw-wash0", and "tw-wash1" are the
definition for TimeWarp group.  An objects in TimeWarp group is
created by the form:
  [object foo
   (group tw-group)
    ...]
Local virtual time for an object can be referred by a pseudo-variable
"LVT".  A message with virtual receive time is sent by a form:
  [<target> <= <message> @ <reply> :vrt <time>]
An object that receives the message is automatically adjusted its
virtual time, or forced to rollback.  When rollback occurs, state
variables of the object is automatically restored.

The author apologizes that this program is very hard-to-read.  We
could write more sophisticated one, if we had more time...

References

[1]  David R. Jefferson, "Virtual Time," in ACM Transactions on
Programming Languages and Systems, 7(3): 404-425, July 1985.

[2] Satoshi Matsuoka, Takuo Watanabe, and Akinori Yonezawa, "Hybrid
Group Reflective Architecture for Object-Oriented Concurrent
Reflective Programming," in Proc. ECOOP'91, number 512 in LNCS,
pp.231-250, Springer-Verlag, 1991

[3] Hidehiko Masuhara, Satoshi Matsuoka, Takuo Watanabe, and Akinori
Yonezawa, "Object-Oriented Concurrent Reflective Languages can be
Implemented Efficiently," in Proc. OOPSLA'92, to appear.
